mp;w=256&amp;q=75 256w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_12667.jpeg&amp;w=384&amp;q=75 384w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_12667.jpeg&amp;w=640&amp;q=75 640w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_12667.jpeg&amp;w=750&amp;q=75 750w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_12667.jpeg&amp;w=828&amp;q=75 828w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_12667.jpeg&amp;w=1080&amp;q=75 1080w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_12667.jpeg&amp;w=1200&amp;q=75 1200w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_12667.jpeg&amp;w=1920&amp;q=75 1920w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_12667.jpeg&amp;w=2048&amp;q=75 2048w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_12667.jpeg&amp;w=3840&amp;q=75 3840w\" src=\"/_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_12667.jpeg&amp;w=3840&amp;q=75\"/></div></div><article class=\"prose-none space-y-4 font-body text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.85] text-ink\"><p>71. Shri Bhaybhanjan Parshwanath</p><p>Post – Bhinmal, District – Jalore (Rajasthan).</p><p>Main Deity: A golden-coloured image of Lord Shri Bhaybhanjan Parshwanath, made of five metals (panchdhatu), in the padmasana posture, 12.5 inches tall and 15 inches wide, without a hood-canopy.</p><p>Location: This is in the city of Bhinmal.</p><p>History: Bhinmal, the chief town of the ancient state of Gujarat, was once extremely famous. There is no definitive historical record of who founded this town. According to legend, its name was Shrimal in the Satya Yuga, Ratnamal in the Treta Yuga, Pushpamal in the Dvapara Yuga, and Bhinmal in the Kali Yuga; the names Shrimal and Bhinmal became the most widely known. This city has risen and fallen many times over the ages. An inscription dated V.S. 1333 has been found among the ruins of a Jain temple, stating that Lord Mahavir once travelled through here. There are also references to Acharya Shri Vajraswami travelling toward Shrimal (Bhinmal) in the first century of the Vikram era. According to a Jain pattavali (lineage record), around Veer Nirvan Samvat 70, in the time of Shri Ratnaprabhsuriji, Prince Shri Sundar and minister Shri Uhad of Shrimal town went from here and founded Osian, to which many families from Shrimal then migrated. According to another account, when King Deshal of Shrimal permitted the wealthy to settle within the fort, others, dissatisfied, went with Deshal&#x27;s son Jaychandra and settled at Osian in V.S. 223. At one time this city had a circumference of 64 km. The fort had 84 gates, among which were the homes of 84 millionaire shravakas, 64 Shrimal Brahmins, and 8 Pragvat Brahmins. This city was made beautiful by hundreds of spired temples. In the &quot;Nishithchurni,&quot; composed by Shri Jindasgani in V.S. 733, this city is described as having been extremely prosperous since before the 7th–8th centuries. From the 7th to the 10th centuries, nearly all the influential acharyas came here, sanctifying this city and composing many precious works of Jain literature, offering the world invaluable and ever-fresh inspiration.</p><p>This city is also given great importance in traditional legends. It is recorded that Lord Shri Mahavir travelled through here. In the first century, Acharya Shri Vajra Swami came here for darshan. Minister Shri Uhad and Prince Sundar went from here and founded the city of Osian. This is also the birthplace of the poet Shri Magh, author of the epic &quot;Shishupal Vadh.&quot; In Samvat 992, Siddhashir Gani composed &quot;Upamiti Bhava Prapancha Katha&quot; here. The ancestors of minister Vimalasha lived here. The Shrimali lineage originated here.</p><p>This city has witnessed many periods of rise and fall. A wealthy merchant built a beautiful and grand temple and installed a five-metal image of Lord Parshwanath in it. Out of fear of Muslim invasion, this image was hidden away. In Vikram Samvat 1651, when excavation was carried out at the temple site, this image was found by the people, together with a samavasarana and several other images. This image of Parshwanath was then installed with great pomp and ceremony in the temple of Lord Shantinath.</p><p>The Subedar (governor) of Bhinmal learned of the appearance of this image. At that time Jalore was under the rule of Ghaznikhan; the Subedar reported this to him. The king decided to break the image and melt it down to make a bell, and had it brought to him. This filled the entire Jain sangh with great anxiety. People tried to persuade the king, but he would not budge. Devout shravakas undertook various vows of abstinence (abhigrah); Virchand Sheth vowed to fast from food. Through the sheth&#x27;s steadfastness, the presiding deity manifested itself. But Ghazikhan, intoxicated with power, refused to hand over the image. He summoned a goldsmith and ordered him to make from the image a nine-strand necklace and anklet-bells for his horse, for his wife.</p><p>As soon as the goldsmith attempted to break the image on the king&#x27;s order, the buzzing of hundreds of bees filled the air with dread. Terrible clouds gathered in the sky. Invisible strikes rained down upon the army. Elephants and horses began to die. Many soldiers began to perish.</p><p>The subjects too began urging the king to release the image. Suddenly Ghazikhan collapsed, seized by unbearable pain throughout his body. Utterly terrified, the king consented to hand over the image. He installed it upon a throne and worshipped it with great honour, then handed it over to the sangh. With joy and enthusiasm the sangh brought the image to Bhinmal and installed it amid great celebration. Everyone completed their vows of abstinence. The five sons of the wicked Subedar of Bhinmal, who had given Ghazikhan the false counsel, all died together.</p><p>In V.S. 1662, Sheth Varjang built a magnificent temple with a nine-chowk hall and circumambulatory path. The image of Parshwanath was installed on the full-moon day of Kartik. Because it delivered the people from fear of the king, this image became famous under the name Bhaybhanjan (&quot;Destroyer of Fear&quot;). In V.S. 1671 another temple, of Shri Chandraprabhaswami, was built here. Worshipping this Lord Parshwanath frees people from fear.</p><p>Other Temples: There are 8 more temples in the city and 2 outside it, all dating from the 14th to the 18th centuries. A new temple has been built in Bhinmal — the &quot;Shri Lakshmi Vallabh Parshwanath 72 Jinalaya Mahatirth&quot; — a very beautiful and artistic temple built on 100 acres of land. The Lord&#x27;s image was installed here in February 2011.</p><p>Art and Sculpture: The ruins of old buildings in this ancient and famous city of Bhinmal are full of artistry. There are many ancient temples here with artistic images, truly delightful to behold.</p><p>Directions: Bhinmal railway station is 1 km away, on the Bhildi–Jalore railway line. Dharamshala and bhojanshala facilities are available here, along with a treasure trove of Jain manuscripts. Bus service and private vehicles are available from Jalore and Sirohi.</p><p>Scripture References: This shrine is mentioned in &quot;Nishidh Churni,&quot; &quot;Shrifal Siddhanath,&quot; &quot;Upamati Bhava Prapancha Katha,&quot; &quot;Shri Uttaradhyaya Vritti,&quot; &quot;Tirtha Vandana Stotra,&quot; &quot;Vimal Prabandh,&quot; &quot;Tirthmala,&quot; &quot;365 Shri Parshwa Jin Nammala,&quot; &quot;Shri Shankheshwar Parshwanath Chhand,&quot; &quot;Shri Bhinmal Stavan,&quot; &quot;Shri Parshwanath Nammala,&quot; &quot;Chaitya Paripati,&quot; and others.</p><p>Hymn of Praise: O Lord, you showed a miracle to humble the pride of Ghazikhan; you are the God of Bhinmal, who broke the fear that threatened the sangh; break also the fear born of my own delusion, as I meditate upon you. &quot;With loving devotion I bow in reverence to Shri Bhaybhanjan Parshwa.&quot;</p><p>Chanting Mantra: Om Hreem Arham Shri Bhaybhanjan Parshwanathaya Namah.</p><p>Trust: Shri Bhaybhanjan Parshwanath Shwetambar Jain Trust, Hathiyon ki Pol, Post: Bhinmal, District: Jalore, State: Rajasthan – 343029, India. Phone: 02969-221190, Mobile: 9461132192.</p></article><nav class=\"mt-14 flex items-center justify-between border-t border-sandstone-deep/60 pt-6\"><a class=\"group flex items-center gap-1.5 font-ui text-sm text-ink-soft hover:text-maroon transition-colors\" href=\"/en/tirtha/tirtha-088\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" class=\"lucide lucide-chevron-left h-4 w-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"m15 18-6-6 6-6\"></path></svg><span class=\"max-w-[10rem] truncate sm:max-w-xs\">Shri Ashapuran Parshwanath</span></a><a class=\"group flex items-center gap-1.5 text-right font-ui text-sm text-ink-soft hover:text-maroon transition-colors\" href=\"/en/tirtha/tirtha-090\"><span class=\"max-w-[10rem] truncate sm:max-w-xs\">Shri Jiravala Parshwanath</span><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" class=\"lucide lucide-chevron-right h-4 w-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><pat